<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Valley of the Kings</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1539–1075 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century-bce">16th century BCE</a> · Also known as Valley of the Gates of the Kings</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Valley of the Kings is a wadi on the west bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>, opposite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a> (modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxor">Luxor</a>), where rock-cut tombs were excavated for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a> and powerful nobles of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> for nearly 500 years, from the Eighteenth to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twentieth-dynasty-of-egypt">Twentieth Dynasty</a>. It holds 65 known tombs and chambers, from the simple pit KV54 to KV5, with over 120 chambers for the sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a>; only about twenty contain the remains of kings. Almost all were robbed in antiquity, and during the civil war under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-xi">Ramesses XI</a> the mummies were gathered into caches. The discovery of the tomb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tutankhamun">Tutankhamun</a> in 1922 drew wide attention to the valley, which became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1979.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/valley-of-the-kings.jpg" alt="Valley of the Kings" width="330" height="232" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fotograf/Photographer: Peter J. Bubenik (1995) · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luxor,_Tal_der_Könige_(1995,_860x605).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The walls of the royal tombs in the <b>Valley of the Kings</b> were covered with books of the underworld, such as the Amduat and the Book of Gates, which map the sun’s night journey.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-egyptian-religion">Ancient Egyptian religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>…Deir el-Medina, the “servants in the Place of Truth”, who cut and decorated the royal tombs in the <b>Valley of the Kings</b>; their village dates from the time of Thutmose I. They lived with their families in a walled village of about 68 houses and worked in two…</blockquote><a href="/societies/ancient-egypt">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Egypt</a></li><li><blockquote>Howard Carter's workers uncovered a step cut into the rock of the <b>Valley of the Kings</b>, leading to the nearly intact tomb of Tutankhamun.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-4">4 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
